<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Part III &mdash; Seams and Return &middot; Inverted-Torus Universe</p>
  <h1>The Universe of Inverted Tori</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">Perhaps one universe-instance is not merely made from inverted tori. Perhaps that
    universe is the Möbius seam itself: one globally twisted return whose local cross-sections are ordinary
    tori, and whose completed circuit brings every transported frame back inverted.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The proposal in one sentence</p>
    <h2 id="t-proposal">One Seam, Seen as Many Tori</h2>
    <p class="lede">Let the universe be one globally twisted return. Its apparently separate inverted tori
      are local cross-sections of that return, not rooms outside one another; the Möbius seam is the whole
      relation that carries each slice into the next.</p>
    <p>In a discrete first picture, call the slices \(T_i^2\), each carrying the two ordinary torus angles
      \((u_i,v_i)\). Transport from slice \(i\) to slice \(j\) acts on their winding lattice by an integer
      matrix:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      A_{ij}\in GL(2,\mathbb Z),
      \qquad
      \det A_{ij}=\begin{cases}
      +1,&amp;\text{orientation preserved},\\
      -1,&amp;\text{orientation inverted}.
      \end{cases}
    \]</p>
    <p>The discrete diagram is a connected family of these slices,</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \mathcal U_{\mathrm{inv}}
      =\left(\bigsqcup_iT_i^2\right)\big/\{A_{ij}\},
    \]</p>
    <p>where the quotient sign means that selected loops or channels are identified by the seam maps.
      This writes the idea cleanly; it does not show that physical spacetime is made this way.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading on this page: <span class="sci">&#9670; established mathematics or
      physics</span> &middot; <span class="spec">&#9670; a conditional geometric mechanism</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; the framework&rsquo;s cosmological or philosophical reading</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Before the cosmology</p>
    <h2 id="t-meanings">Four Meanings of &ldquo;Inverted Torus&rdquo;</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">They are not interchangeable. Only one of them changes the structure carried around
      a loop.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>One &mdash; orientation reversal</h3>
      <p class="formula">\[
        I(u,v)=(-u,v),
        \qquad \det(DI)=-1.
      \]</p>
      <p>The poloidal or toroidal coordinate changes handedness. This is the precise meaning used in the
        proposed seam network. The torus remains a torus; what changes is how a local frame is identified
        after crossing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Two &mdash; an inside-out embedding</h3>
      <p>A doughnut-shaped surface can be drawn or deformed so that what looked inward appears outward. If its
        intrinsic distances and connections are unchanged, this may alter only its embedding in surrounding
        space. A new picture is not automatically a new universe.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Three &mdash; reversed circulation</h3>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \mathbf J_{\mathrm{tor}}\mapsto-\mathbf J_{\mathrm{tor}},
        \qquad
        \mathbf J_{\mathrm{pol}}\mapsto-\mathbf J_{\mathrm{pol}}.
      \]</p>
      <p>The currents or field lines flow the other way around the same geometry. That is a dynamical
        inversion, not a topological one. It requires an equation that determines the flow.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Four &mdash; source and sink exchanged</h3>
      <p>A process that formerly expelled through the axis and returned around the body is read in reverse:
        the axis receives and the body redistributes. This is the strongest philosophical image, but it does
        not follow from \(\det(DI)=-1\) alone.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">One more distinction</p>
    <h2 id="t-t2-t3">A Doughnut Surface Is Not a Three-Torus Universe</h2>
    <p>The familiar torus is a two-dimensional surface \(T^2=S^1\times S^1\), often pictured as a doughnut
      embedded in three-dimensional space. A compact spatial cosmology commonly called a toroidal universe is
      instead a three-torus:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      T^3=S^1\times S^1\times S^1.
    \]</p>
    <p>It need not look like a doughnut from any external room. Opposite faces of a fundamental cell are
      identified, and the space has no physical boundary. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> A universe
      <em>of</em> inverted tori is a third proposal again: many toroidal process-cells joined into a network.
      The page studies that network without pretending the three meanings are one result.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The stronger proposal</p>
    <h2 id="t-seam-world">The Universe Is the Möbius Seam</h2>
    <p class="lede">The tori need not be separate rooms joined by a seam. They can be successive fibres of
      one globally twisted universe. On this reading, the universe does not <em>contain</em> the transition.
      The whole universe is the transition.</p>
    <p>Choose an orientation-reversing automorphism \(f:T^2\to T^2\), represented on winding numbers by
      \(A\in GL(2,\mathbb Z)\) with \(\det A=-1\). Form its mapping torus:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \mathcal M_f
      =\frac{T^2\times[0,1]}
      {(x,1)\sim(f(x),0)},
      \qquad \det A=-1.
    \]</p>
    <p>Every slice \(T^2\times\{s\}\) is an ordinary orientable torus. Yet travelling once around the base
      circle identifies the final slice with the first through \(f\), so a carried orientation returns
      reversed. This is the higher-dimensional analogue of constructing a Möbius strip from an interval
      whose ends are glued after reflection:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \mathsf{M\ddot{o}bius}
      =\frac{[-1,1]\times[0,1]}
      {(x,1)\sim(-x,0)}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Replace the interval fibre with a torus fibre and the local strip with a toroidal world. The resulting
      three-dimensional space is globally non-orientable even though every fibre is locally ordinary. A
      spacetime version can keep causal time separate:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \mathcal X=\mathbb R_t\times\mathcal M_f.
    \]</p>
    <p>This matters conceptually. A seam drawn at \(s=0\) is only the cut used to display one fundamental
      domain. It can be moved by choosing another cut. No observer need encounter a glowing wall: the
      inversion is global holonomy, discovered only after a complete circuit. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span>
      Calling this construction our universe remains speculation. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The oriented double cover</p>
    <h2 id="t-two-turns">Twice Around Restores the Frame</h2>
    <p>One circuit applies \(f\) and reverses orientation. Two circuits apply \(f^2\), whose determinant is
      positive:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \det(A^2)=(\det A)^2=+1.
    \]</p>
    <p>The two-turn cover therefore restores orientation. What looked like two opposed toroidal domains can
      be read as the two sheets of one oriented cover of the seam-world, rather than two independent
      universes. This is the exact place where the earlier image of &ldquo;inverted tori&rdquo; and the user&rsquo;s
      stronger proposal meet: the tori are local slices; the Möbius identification is the universe.</p>
  </div>
</section>

