<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">What survives a complete journey</p>
    <h2 id="t-holonomy">The Ledger of Inversions</h2>
    <p>Let a closed route \(\gamma\) cross the seams \(A_1,A_2,\ldots,A_n\). Its orientation holonomy is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \chi(\gamma)=\prod_{k=1}^{n}\operatorname{sgn}\det A_k
      \in\{+1,-1\}.
    \]</p>
    <p>If \(\chi=+1\), the route returns with its orientation restored. If \(\chi=-1\), it returns inverted.
      This is ordinary parity bookkeeping once the seam maps have been chosen. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span>
      The cosmological choice is to let this sign affect what a returning process can become.</p>
    <p>The winding memory of the ordinary torus, \((p,q)\in\mathbb Z^2\), now acquires a third entry:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \mathcal L(\gamma)=(p,q,\chi).
    \]</p>
    <p>The first two numbers remember how often the path circulated. The sign remembers whether the whole
      journey returned its frame or reversed it. In the language of the framework, an inverted-torus universe
      is a cosmos in which return keeps count of both <em>history</em> and <em>handedness</em>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The Prime Being in this instance</p>
    <h2 id="t-prime">How a Prime Residue Would Move Through the Network</h2>
    <p>The companion essay assigns each universe-instance \(u\) one Prime Being \(p(u)\), while allowing every
      being to occupy that role in some other instance. If this particular instance is the mapping-torus
      seam-world \(\mathcal M_f\), its globally transmissible residue inherits the monodromy ledger:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      R^{(u)}_{\gamma}
      =\chi(\gamma)\,\mathcal T_{p,q}R^{(u)}_0,
    \]</p>
    <p>where \(\mathcal T_{p,q}\) transports the residue through the two toroidal winding directions. A route
      with \(\chi=-1\) does not destroy the residue. It presents the residue to the next receiver in an
      orientation-reversed form. A later route with another odd inversion can restore it.</p>
    <p>That gives philosophical distribution a geometry. One receiver may formulate the residue as outward
      creation; another, after an odd seam, as inward return. One may speak of being; another of emptiness.
      Their propositions need not be identical for the transport to preserve a relation between them.
      <span class="myth">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted">The equation is a proposed bookkeeping law. It does not establish that thoughts carry a
      measurable parity or that philosophical disagreement is caused by topology.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Three different cosmologies</p>
    <h2 id="t-two-models">A Network, a Multiverse, or the Seam Itself?</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid three">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Model A &mdash; one networked universe</h3>
      <p>Many toroidal cells inhabit one connected spacetime. Inverting seams are transitions between
        domains. A residue traverses the network and collects \((p,q,\chi)\).</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \mathcal U=\bigcup_iT_i^2/\!\sim
      \]</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Model B &mdash; each torus an instance</h3>
      <p>Each toroidal whole is a separate universe with its own Prime Being. No exchange occurs unless a
        higher portal joins the instances.</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \{\mathcal U_u^{\chi_u}\},
        \quad \chi_u\in\{+1,-1\}.
      \]</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Model C &mdash; the universe is the seam</h3>
      <p>The tori are fibres of one mapping torus. There is no external join between worlds: global return
        itself performs the inversion.</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \mathcal U=\mathcal M_f,
        \quad \det f_*=-1.
      \]</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <p class="lede">The new proposal selects Model C. &ldquo;A universe of inverted toruses&rdquo; becomes a local
        description of one Möbius seam-world: many toroidal slices, one globally twisted whole.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">What inversion does not buy</p>
    <h2 id="t-not-time">Handedness Reversal Is Not Time Reversal</h2>
    <p>Changing orientation does not make entropy decrease. The transformations</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      (u,v,t)\mapsto(-u,v,t)
      \qquad\text{and}\qquad
      (u,v,t)\mapsto(u,v,-t)
    \]</p>
    <p>are different. The first reverses one spatial or internal coordinate. The second reverses the time
      parameter. An inverted torus can still have ordinary causal order, ordinary record formation and an
      increasing thermodynamic entropy. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>The <a href="/temporal-antipodes/">temporal-antipode conjecture</a> is therefore an additional
      hypothesis, not a consequence of \(\det A=-1\). It assigns the two scale-infinities to opposite temporal
      asymptotes. A spatial Möbius return does not give those endpoints a shared clock, and a temporal gluing
      capable of doing so would need its own causal and conservation law. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>Nor does orientation reversal manufacture matter, antimatter or net baryon number. A reversible seam
      can separate, relabel or hide a pre-existing bias, but an exactly symmetric state remains globally
      symmetric unless additional dynamics or asymmetric boundary conditions intervene:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      B_{\mathrm{total}}=0
      \quad\not\xrightarrow{\;\det A=-1\;}\quad
      B_{\mathrm{total}}\neq0.
    \]</p>
    <p>The seam could correlate an already meaningful charge with \(\chi\), or suppress contact between
      sectors. It cannot replace baryogenesis merely by turning the diagram inside out.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The philosophical reading</p>
    <h2 id="t-inside-out">Every Outside Is Another Cell&rsquo;s Inside</h2>
    <p>An ordinary torus already frustrates a simple opposition between departure and return: a path can do
      both. The seam-world goes further. Completing the global circuit can exchange the meaning of inward and
      outward while preserving continuity. What one local slice experiences as emission, another can receive
      as origin.</p>
    <p>This permits a philosophy in which no viewpoint owns the absolute exterior and no point owns the seam.
      Every world-picture has a hole through which it depends on another circulation. The Prime Being is not
      enthroned outside the structure; in this instance it is the role whose residue survives the global
      twist. In another universe-instance, another being and another monodromy may hold that place.</p>
    <p class="lede">An inverted-torus universe would not abolish perspective. It would make perspective part
      of the transport law.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">What the speculation owes</p>
    <h2 id="t-tests">Five Edges That Could Make It More Than a Picture</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>One &mdash; identify the cells</h3>
      <p>Say what physical degree of freedom is toroidal and at what scale. A drawn doughnut is not yet a
        domain of spacetime, matter or mind.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Two &mdash; identify the monodromy</h3>
      <p>If the universe is the seam, do not search for a wall. Specify the global return map
        \(f_*\in GL(2,\mathbb Z)\) and show what measurable frame or state is reversed after one circuit.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Three &mdash; derive the dynamics</h3>
      <p>Explain why energy or information follows the toroidal winding and how conservation laws behave at
        an inverted seam.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Four &mdash; distinguish coordinates</h3>
      <p>Find an invariant consequence. If every inversion disappears under a relabelling of coordinates,
        the proposed new universe is the old one described backwards.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Five &mdash; look for recurrence</h3>
      <p>A compact cosmic topology may produce repeated or correlated structures. No such observation is
        supplied here; absence of a signature must constrain the scale or reject the model.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>The clean failure</h3>
      <p>If the seam parity has no invariant effect, if transport violates known causal constraints, or if
        the network predicts repetitions the sky excludes, this physical version fails.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2 id="t-ledger">Earned, Chosen, Refused</h2>
    <dl class="glossary">
      <dt>Earned</dt>
      <dd>A torus has two independent winding directions and fundamental group \(\mathbb Z^2\). Its winding
        lattice admits orientation-reversing maps in \(GL(2,\mathbb Z)\). The mapping torus of such a map is
        locally a torus bundle and globally non-orientable; its two-turn cover restores orientation. A
        two-torus surface, a three-torus cosmology, a torus network and a mapping-torus seam-world are distinct
        constructions.</dd>
      <dt>Chosen</dt>
      <dd>That one universe-instance is the mapping torus \(\mathcal M_f\) itself; that its monodromy acts on
        a Prime Being&rsquo;s residue; that source and sink can exchange roles; and that orientation-reversed
        receptions appear as contrasting philosophical ideas.</dd>
      <dt>Refused</dt>
      <dd>Calling an inside-out drawing new physics. Treating the displayed cut as a literal wall. Treating
        parity reversal as time reversal. Claiming that inversion creates matter, antimatter or baryon
        asymmetry. Confusing \(T^2\) with \(T^3\). Using global topology to escape every observation.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame card card--linked illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The local-throat question</p>
    <h2 id="t-wormhole-bridge"><a class="card-link" href="/wormholes/">When Does an Inverted Torus Become a Wormhole?</a></h2>
    <p>The mapping-torus universe above needs no local portal. A stronger model would identify two mouths,
      supply a Lorentzian throat, solve for its stress&ndash;energy, survive perturbations and preserve causal
      order. The dedicated page separates those gravitational debts from global orientation holonomy.</p>
