<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Closing stance</p>
    <p class="lede">Perhaps the universe of inverted tori is the Möbius seam itself: not many doughnuts behind
      many gates, but one torus-fibred return whose global identification reverses every carried frame.</p>
    <p>Such a universe would let one residue remain continuous without remaining unchanged. It would make
      handedness part of memory, turn inside and outside into local relations, and leave no privileged wall
      called <em>the seam</em>. The seam would be the topology of the whole instance through which its Prime
      Being is distributed.</p>
    <p>The newer <a href="/little-red-dots/">negative-interval conjecture</a> borrows this orientation-reversing
      grammar but adds something this topology does not supply: passage from a prior closure through
      \((-1,0)\) into measurable positive-side seeds. Its
      <a href="/little-red-dots/research-proposal/">research proposal</a> must therefore derive transport and
      conservation separately; a mapping space alone cannot make matter cross. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Begin with <a href="/torus/">the ordinary torus</a>, follow the half-twist through
      <a href="/moebius/">the Möbius seam</a>, and compare the one-prime-per-instance proposal in
      <a href="/time-and-residue/">The One Residue That Travels</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>
